items.raw:
We have $g^+=2$ if, and only if,   $p=11$ and, thus, the curve $X_0^+(11^2)$ is hyperelliptic. For $p>11$,  on has $g^+>2$   and, moreover, $p>13$ because  $X_0^+(13^2)$ is a plane quartic (cf. \cite{Baran}).  Lemma 2.5 of \cite{BGGP} states that there is a basis $f_1,\cdots, f_{g^+}$ of $S_2(\Gamma_0(p^2))^{\langle w_{p^2}\rangle}$ with rational $q$-expansions satisfying
\begin{equation}\label{congq}
f_i(q)=\left\{\begin{array}{cr} q^i+ O(q^i)&\text{ if the cusp $\infty$ is not a Weierstrass point of $X_0^+ (p^2)$,}\\q^{2 i-1}+ O(q^{2i-1})& \text{otherwise.}\end{array} \right.
\end{equation}

